## Project Title

Hospital Queue Management System

## Problem Statement

Hospitals handle a large number of patients every day, making efficient queue management essential for reducing waiting time and maintaining an organized consultation process. Traditional manual queue systems can be time-consuming and may make it difficult to properly prioritize emergency cases and senior citizens.

The objective of this project is to design and develop a Hospital Queue Management System using Java programming. The system provides a digital platform where patients can be registered, assigned to hospital departments, provided with tokens, and managed through a priority-based queue system.

The system uses three priority levels:

- Priority 1 – Emergency
- Priority 2 – Senior Citizen (60+)
- Priority 3 – Normal

Emergency patients are given the highest priority, followed by senior citizens and then normal patients.

The system is designed using Object-Oriented Programming principles, where components such as patients, departments, queues, and priority management are organized into separate modules. This improves code readability, maintainability, and scalability.

The application also includes input validation and error handling to prevent invalid operations and ensure reliable queue management.

Through this project, Java concepts such as classes, objects, encapsulation, ArrayList, collections, methods, conditional statements, loops, and exception handling are applied to develop a practical hospital management solution.

## Project Scope

The scope of the Hospital Queue Management System is to develop a prototype hospital queue application that performs essential patient registration and queue management operations through a console-based interface.

The system covers the following areas:

- Registration of new patients.
- Generation of unique patient IDs.
- Generation of department-wise token numbers.
- Selection of hospital departments.
- Emergency patient identification.
- Automatic priority assignment.
- Management of department-wise queues.
- Displaying patient and queue information.
- Calling the next patient for consultation.
- Completing patient consultations.
- Cancelling patient tokens.
- Searching for patient records.
- Displaying queue statistics.
- Input validation and error handling.

The project mainly focuses on implementing the core functionalities required for hospital queue management. It does not aim to replace a complete hospital management platform but acts as a learning-oriented implementation that demonstrates the practical use of Java programming.

The system can be further expanded by integrating database management systems, graphical user interfaces, online appointment booking, doctor management, notification systems, cloud services, and mobile applications.

## Target Users

The Hospital Queue Management System is designed for:

### Hospital Staff

Receptionists and hospital staff who need to register patients, generate tokens, and manage department-wise queues.

### Doctors and Medical Staff

Medical staff who need to call patients for consultation and update their consultation status.

### Patients

Patients who need to register themselves and receive a token for consultation.

### Students and Developers

The project serves as an educational example for understanding Java programming, Object-Oriented Programming, collections, and queue management.

### Small Clinics

Small clinics and hospitals can use the system as a prototype for managing basic patient queues.

## High-Level Features

### 1. Patient Management Module

This module manages patient registration and patient information.

Features include:

- Registering new patients.
- Storing patient information.
- Generating unique patient IDs.
- Recording patient name, age, gender, and contact information.
- Selecting the required department.
- Identifying emergency patients.

### 2. Token Management Module

This module manages the generation and assignment of patient tokens.

Features include:

- Generating sequential token numbers.
- Maintaining separate token sequences for different departments.
- Displaying token information.
- Associating tokens with patients.
- Tracking token status.

### 3. Queue Management Module

This module manages patients waiting for consultation.

Features include:

- Maintaining department-wise queues.
- Displaying waiting patients.
- Calculating queue positions.
- Calling the next patient.
- Managing waiting and active patients.
- Preventing multiple active consultations in the same department.

### 4. Priority Management Module

This module determines the order in which patients are served.

The priority system is:

1. Emergency – Highest Priority
2. Senior Citizen (60+) – Medium Priority
3. Normal – Standard Priority

The system automatically assigns priority based on emergency status and patient age.

Patients with the same priority are served according to their token number.

### 5. Consultation Management Module

This module manages the consultation status of patients.

The consultation workflow is:

Waiting → In Progress → Completed

Features include:

- Calling the next patient.
- Marking a patient as In Progress.
- Completing a consultation.
- Cancelling a token.
- Tracking completed consultations.

### 6. Department Management Module

The system supports multiple hospital departments.

The available departments are:

- General Medicine
- Cardiology
- Orthopedics
- Pediatrics
- ENT
- Emergency

Each department maintains its own patient queue.

### 7. Validation and Error Handling Module

This module ensures accurate and reliable system operation.

Features include:

- Validating patient names.
- Validating age input.
- Checking gender information.
- Validating department selection.
- Preventing invalid queue operations.
- Handling empty queues.
- Preventing multiple patients from being served simultaneously in one department.
- Displaying appropriate error messages.

## Technical Approach

The project is developed using Java and follows Object-Oriented Programming methodology.

The implementation includes:

- Classes and objects to represent patients, departments, and queue management components.
- Encapsulation for organizing and protecting object data.
- ArrayList collections for storing patient records.
- Java Collections for managing and organizing queues.
- Priority-based queue logic for determining service order.
- Methods for implementing reusable operations.
- Conditional statements for priority determination and validation.
- Loops for menu-driven interaction and data processing.
- Exception handling for reliable execution.
- Modular programming for separating different responsibilities.
- Command-line interface for terminal-based execution.
- GitHub for version control and project management.

## Expected Outcome

The expected outcome of this project is a functional Hospital Queue Management System that successfully performs patient registration and queue management through an interactive command-line interface.

The system allows hospital staff to register patients, generate tokens, assign priorities, manage department-wise queues, call patients, complete consultations, cancel tokens, and view queue information.

The priority-based system ensures that emergency patients are handled first, followed by senior citizens and normal patients according to the defined queue rules.

The project demonstrates how Java programming and Object-Oriented Programming concepts can be applied to solve a real-world hospital queue management problem.

The system also provides practical experience in software design, Java programming, data structures, collections, validation, exception handling, testing, and GitHub-based project management.

The project provides a foundation for future development into a more advanced hospital management solution with database integration, graphical interfaces, online appointments, doctor management, notifications, and cloud-based services.
